            0.058
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atomic Foundations of Matter"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 169"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.089,
            0.44,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.4 How Atoms Combine?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.114,
            0.775,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Atoms of an element can combine to form a molecule of that element. For example, a hydrogen molecule consists of two hydrogen atoms. Atoms of different elements combine to form a molecule of a compound. For example, one hydrogen atom and one chlorine atom combine to form a molecule of hydrogen chloride. A molecule can be defined as an electrically neutral entity consisting of more than one atom that is capable of independent existence and shows all the properties of that substance. It should also be remembered that some elements, such as helium, exist only as atoms because its atoms are stable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.267,
            0.775,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In Chapter 8, Journey Inside the Atom, you learnt that atoms with 8 electrons in the outermost (valence) shell (2 electrons if the K-shell is the outermost shell) are stable. If the number of electrons in a valence shell is less than eight, then they may share, gain or lose electrons to complete their valence shell and become stable. In this process, atoms of elements combine to form compounds. This generally takes place in two ways. These are:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.387,
            0.775,
            0.421
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sharing of electrons —",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Share a few or all of their valence electrons with another atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.424,
            0.775,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "y "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Transfer of electrons —",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Transfer one or more of the valence electrons to another atom, or accept one or more electrons from some other atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.462,
            0.775,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "When atoms combine, the total energy of the system becomes lower than the sum of the energies of the individual atoms, making the resulting arrangement more stable. The force that holds atoms together is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "chemical bond",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Let us explore it further."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.539,
            0.715,
            0.56
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9.4.1 Bonding by sharing of electrons — Covalent Bond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.565,
            0.352,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A. Molecules of elements",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.587,
            0.775,
            0.621
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us understand the formation of molecules of elements by the sharing of electrons, as described below."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.624,
            0.512,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "molecule:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.662,
            0.513,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Write the electronic configuration of hydrogen (atomic number 1). It has only one electron in the K-shell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.536,
            0.609,
            0.961,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 14,
              "bbox": [
                0.536,
                0.609,
                0.961,
                0.683
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_14.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.532,
                0.687,
                0.645,
                0.702
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hydrogen atom"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 16,
              "bbox": [
                0.667,
                0.688,
                0.779,
                0.703
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hydrogen atom"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image_footnote",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.808,
                0.687,
                0.949,
                0.702
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Hydrogen molecule"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.596,
            0.709,
            0.904,
            0.724
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "g. Fi 9.6: Formation of a hydrogen molecule"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.717,
            0.63,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since, a K-shell can have a total of two electrons, it needs one more electron to become stable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.754,
            0.775,
            0.857
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thus, a hydrogen atom shares one electron with another hydrogen atom to form a hydrogen molecule (H ), as shown in Fig. 9.6. The shared pair of electrons attracts both the nuclei and makes the molecule stable. This type of interaction between atoms through a shared pair of electrons is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "covalent bond",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". If two atoms are joined by sharing one electron each, they are said to be joined by a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "single bond",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.858,
            0.774,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This is generally depicted by drawing a single line between the symbols of the two atoms as H—H."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.896,
            0.524,
            0.914
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us take the case of a chlorine molecule."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
